{"t": 1795307748.4300072, "event": "text", "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "text": "The two networks are **\u03b5-equivalent (SAFE)** for \u03b5 = 0.339949 on the box [\u22121,1]\u2074. The proof tree has **3503 nodes** (1752 leaves, all closed, no lemmas), and the SAFE certificate is written to `/tmp/claude-0/bench_claude3/safe_hard/certificate.json`.\n\n**Weight report**\n- **Where the difference is:** every layer changed, everywhere. The weight differences are dense noise in all entries and of full rank: about 8%, 6%, 7% and 12% of the weights' size in the four affine layers. Bias differences are below 0.008. There is no sparse, low-rank or sign-consistent part to target.\n- **First layer:** net1 has zero first-layer biases and so does the difference, and the box is centred on the origin. So all 24 first-layer hyperplanes pass through the origin and every unit changes phase inside the box.\n- **Hidden layers:** on the full box, all 24 units in the second hidden layer are unstable in both networks (24 of 24 relaxed for the difference too). The difference before that layer's ReLU spans about \u00b10.5 per unit, so no unit passes the difference through linearly.\n- **Outputs:** sampled distances are 0.044 at the origin, 0.10 at (1,1,1,1) and 0.18 at (\u22121,1,\u22121,1). The bound on the whole box was 3.17, about 10 times \u03b5.\n\n**Hypotheses and what came of them**\n1. *All first-layer hyperplanes meet at the origin, so cutting at 0 is the natural phase boundary.* I split at x\u2082 = 0. This helps only a little: inside each half, the hyperplanes still cross.\n2. *Lemmas won't pay off.* With dense noise in every layer, a bound on the hidden-layer difference would have to be tight on every unit at once. Proving it would need roughly the same input refinement as the main proof, so I introduced no lemmas.\n3. *Even refinement suits a dense perturbation.* Because the difference is spread over all inputs, splitting the widest dimension shrinks every input term evenly. The tree built this way had 3503 nodes. A first attempt with VeryDiff's default heuristic and no manual split had 3763 nodes; I discarded it after saving it as a backup.\n\nThe proof needed fine splitting: a region of 1/128 of the box still had a bound of about 1.38, against \u03b5 \u2248 0.34. I found no structural shortcut, so I don't expect a much smaller tree with these tools. The exploration sandbox was down (Docker wasn't running), so I computed nothing outside the kernel; the point checks above used the kernel's own point evaluation."}
